{"candidates":[{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.resetPassword","start_line":367,"end_line":420,"explanation":"I inferred this from the symbol list and my knowledge of this boilerplate. I did not see the method body. The reset hash is a stateless JWT. resetPassword only verifies the signature and expiry, reads forgotUserId, and sets the new password. Nothing marks the token as used, and nothing ties it to the user's current password or state. The same link therefore stays valid until it expires and can be replayed."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.forgotPassword","start_line":319,"end_line":365,"explanation":"This method signs the reset JWT with only the user id and a global forgot secret. The token carries no per-user or one-time component, such as a password-hash fingerprint or a stored token id. That is why it cannot be invalidated once used."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.getForgotSecret","start_line":588,"end_line":595,"explanation":"The signing and verification secret is a static config value. It does not change after the password changes, so earlier tokens still verify after a successful reset."}],"fix":{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","description":"Make reset tokens single use. Option 1: in forgotPassword, sign the JWT with a per-user secret, for example forgotSecret + user.password hash (or password hash + updatedAt). In resetPassword, decode the token to get forgotUserId, load the user, then verifyAsync with the same per-user secret. Once the password changes, the old token fails verification. Option 2: store a hashed one-time token with an expiry on the user or in a separate table. Look it up in resetPassword, then delete it when the password is set. In both cases, keep deleting the user's sessions after the reset."}}
